WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:02.151
Welcome back to the David Pakman show. It's
great to welcome to the program today, Dr

00:00:02.151 --> 00:00:07.480
Marty Macquarrie, who's a Johns Hopkins surgeon
and a New York Times bestselling author. His

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:12.700
book, the price we pay, what broke American
healthcare and how to fix it is available

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:18.810
now on Amazon. Um, I think a good place to
start is what did break American healthcare.

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:24.470
I mean, I'm former Cigna executive. Wendell
Potter writes in his very good book, deadly

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:29.119
spin about how in the period between World
War One and World War II, the u s was sort

00:00:29.119 --> 00:00:33.220
of headed in the direction of a system, more
like many of the European systems. But at

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:39.660
the time it seemed to German when we were
not getting along with Germany, that plan

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:45.320
was abandoned and that was sort of the beginning
of the plan and the system that we have now.

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:48.600
How do you see the crux of how we got to where
we are now?

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:53.880
Well, people have a lot of opinions, David,
on healthcare and everybody has their own

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:59.230
experience or their interface. Even doctors,
we tend to see it from our vantage point in

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:06.720
our specialty. But if we look broadly at the
entire system and we actually are not beholden

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:13.469
to any particular special interest or stakeholder
and can talk openly and honestly with a critical

00:01:13.469 --> 00:01:19.729
view of the entire system, it's really two
fundamental root problems. Number one, pricing

00:01:19.729 --> 00:01:27.880
failures and number two, inappropriate care
or the appropriateness of care. If we look

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:34.649
at the loss of competition in healthcare and
how, um, you can't get a price for basic services,

00:01:34.649 --> 00:01:41.170
people have a right to be angry. And when
those services, 20% of the time are unnecessary,

00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:46.279
which is what our research from Johns Hopkins
has shown in a national survey of US doctors,

00:01:46.279 --> 00:01:54.340
they say 21% on average of all medical cares,
unnecessary testing, even procedures, medications.

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:58.890
These are the two fundamental areas that we
can improve. And in the book, the price we

00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:04.789
pay, I tried to find the disrupters nationally
who are actually fixing health care by addressing

00:02:04.789 --> 00:02:07.430
needs to underlying root problem.

00:02:07.430 --> 00:02:12.610
Now there are different philosophies that
would have one person here, what you just

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:18.420
laid out and go in the direction of, well,
the solution would be total free market for

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:23.450
profit, let everybody shop and do exactly
whatever it is they want. Let the sort of

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:28.170
laissez faire approach fix the problem. And
there are others who would hear what you said

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:32.830
and say, Oh, no, no, no. We need a completely
different system. We need one actually, uh,

00:02:32.830 --> 00:02:39.290
where we can have bigger, uh, parties negotiating
on our behalf up to and including the government

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:44.020
through some kind of single payer system.
Which does one or the other strike you as

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:47.480
the best path forward to solve the two problems
you outlined?

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:53.150
Well, it'd be very honest, David, I actually
think that there's tremendous consensus in

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:59.100
the United States if we can lay out the issues
for what they really are, that price gouging

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:05.490
is wrong and that that price gouging happens
today and it will continue to happen if the

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:09.710
government is the payer. And by the way, the
government does not have a great track record

00:03:09.710 --> 00:03:15.030
as being a very good negotiator. When you
deliver a baby in Boston, you can have that

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:23.140
done for $6,000 or $41,000 both at Harvard
hospitals. There's no difference in quality.

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:28.940
These are some times the same doctors. Price
Gouging has sadly become a way of doing business.

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:34.710
And if we can get rid of the waste in the
system, that entire spend that makes a lot

00:03:34.710 --> 00:03:42.490
of people rich and care but is wasted money,
we could create an efficient marketplaces

00:03:42.490 --> 00:03:47.540
and actually cut the waste in a way that's
bipartisan and it retains the private health

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:52.660
insurance system and at the same time keeps
the healthy Medicare government payer system

00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:53.660
going strong.

00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:58.330
So it sounds like you're saying you wouldn't
necessarily want to expand any government

00:03:58.330 --> 00:04:02.380
involvement, you would want to keep Medicare
and maybe medicaid, but you would want to

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:06.730
improve the for profit healthcare system that
we have. Is that what I'm hearing?

00:04:06.730 --> 00:04:12.150
Well, look, I applaud people who find Medicare
for all attractive because they want to get

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:17.290
rid of the waste. The problem is down the
stream longterm 100% of the time in every

00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:22.780
country around the world, including our own
longterm governments, tighten the belt on

00:04:22.780 --> 00:04:28.870
healthcare expenditures and you can be left
with a dilapidated system at 10 or 20 years

00:04:28.870 --> 00:04:33.370
if we want to cut the waste, which is where
I think there's broad consensus, we can do

00:04:33.370 --> 00:04:37.870
that by creating transparency within the system.
I opened the book the price we pay with a

00:04:37.870 --> 00:04:43.200
group of doctors in the Washington DC area
that are gouging Medicare, that are taking

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:48.380
advantage of the system, that are our tax
payers are funding a group of doctors doing

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:53.851
unnecessary procedures all day long. That
will continue with Medicare for all. That

00:04:53.851 --> 00:05:01.280
doesn't stop. So if we, if we just look at
competition and transparency, we can actually

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:05.880
reduce the waste because I personally believe
David, that we already spend enough money

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:09.570
in America to give everybody gold-plated healthcare.

00:05:09.570 --> 00:05:13.690
Oh yeah. There's no question that we do. I
guess I'm not sure that I agree with you because,

00:05:13.690 --> 00:05:18.160
yeah. Okay. So I think if you say to basically
any American, do you want to get rid of waste

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:22.170
in the healthcare system? Basically every
American would say, yes, I do want to get

00:05:22.170 --> 00:05:28.850
rid of waste. The problem is that many Americans
see profit in an area where there is completely

00:05:28.850 --> 00:05:34.290
inelastic demand as part of the waste that
should be removed. There are people who see

00:05:34.290 --> 00:05:40.340
a system that requires medical billing experts
on both the insurance side and the provider

00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:45.740
side as a form of waste, which we could get
rid of through some kind of government centered

00:05:45.740 --> 00:05:50.290
program. So I do agree with you that waste
is bad and Americans want to get rid of it.

00:05:50.290 --> 00:05:54.740
I don't think that there is as much agreement
as maybe you're suggesting as to what constitutes

00:05:54.740 --> 00:05:55.810
that waste though.

00:05:55.810 --> 00:06:01.820
Well, if we can agree that there's a tremendous
amount of waste, if we can agree that walking

00:06:01.820 --> 00:06:08.780
into a New York hospital and having a bill
of $70,000 is agregious when the market price

00:06:08.780 --> 00:06:14.130
is six to seven there's a tremendous amount
of waste there that can be cut without all

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:19.590
of the infighting that's partisan about what's
the great philosophy to dominate healthcare.

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:24.110
These are, this is the low hanging fruit in
the system and trust me, nobody has been more

00:06:24.110 --> 00:06:32.210
aggressive in fighting the profiteering philosophy
and medicine that has taken over and ruin

00:06:32.210 --> 00:06:37.840
the medical profession in certain of America
than me and my team. We go to hospitals, we

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:45.180
call out CEOs. We just did a giant expos say
of hospitals that sue patients in the state

00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:50.800
of Virginia. A study that came out this week
and Jama showed that some hospitals about

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:55.940
10% aggressively take their patients to court
when they can't afford their bill.

00:06:55.940 --> 00:06:59.430
We have called them out and in the hospital,
that was the worst offender announced they're

00:06:59.430 --> 00:07:04.930
going to stop suing patients as a result of
our work in the NPR piece that ensued. So

00:07:04.930 --> 00:07:10.130
we have been calling out people that have
been using prophet to take advantage of people

00:07:10.130 --> 00:07:15.020
all across the country. And you're absolutely
right. It's wrong. It erodes the great public

00:07:15.020 --> 00:07:21.060
trust of our medical profession. Right. Hospitals
were built to be a safe haven, a refuge for

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:26.960
the sick and injured regardless of their race,
creed, or ability to pay. That is the charter

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:32.889
of most us hospitals, many of which were bayed
were built on charity or built by churches

00:07:32.889 --> 00:07:35.460
or by land grants from the community.

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:39.210
Yeah, so let's, I mean, let's focus in on
that a little bit because that may be something

00:07:39.210 --> 00:07:42.780
my audience doesn't know about. I think my
audience probably understands the philosophical

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:47.300
differences sort of underlying the, the idea
of the systems that we're talking about. Talk

00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:54.300
a little bit about the fact that there are
lawsuits against patients by hospitals. Talk

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:59.990
about the wage garnishment. Also that I think
many in my audience have no idea is even a

00:07:59.990 --> 00:08:06.840
thing that hospitals do have mechanisms for
actually garnishing the wages of former patients

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:08.710
for unpaid medical bills.

00:08:08.710 --> 00:08:14.460
Sure. Well, my research team started looking
at the markup on medical bills, identifying

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:20.759
extreme outlier facilities, hospitals, even
physicians who are charging 23 times more

00:08:20.759 --> 00:08:26.040
than what the Medicare allowable amount is.
When we would approach hospitals, CEOs around

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:31.620
the country and say, ask them why are you
charging five times more than the next hospital

00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:36.839
that sees the same case mix and payer mix?
The answer was always, well, we have secretly

00:08:36.839 --> 00:08:42.040
negotiated contracts with insurance. Don't
worry, Marty, nobody pays those bills. So

00:08:42.040 --> 00:08:46.580
we did some research and found out, no, a
lot of people are expected to pay that bill

00:08:46.580 --> 00:08:51.020
in full and those people are expected to pay
that bill and if they don't, they're taken

00:08:51.020 --> 00:08:57.440
to court and sued by a hospital and have their
paycheck garnished or liens put on their homes.

00:08:57.440 --> 00:09:05.420
And we found one hospital that has sued 24,000
people in a town that by census data has 28,000

00:09:05.420 --> 00:09:06.660
people living in it.

00:09:06.660 --> 00:09:11.800
We went to New Mexico and in the book the
price we pay, I visited some of these courthouses

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:17.680
where we found the courthouse transformed
into a taxpayer funded collections agency

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:23.710
for the hospital. Lives ruined. I mean these
are hardworking Americans who's been financially

00:09:23.710 --> 00:09:29.240
devastated, right. Most people don't live
like you and I, David, right. We are relatively

00:09:29.240 --> 00:09:33.190
wealthy in the United States, right? God's
been to me, I have a good life as a surgeon

00:09:33.190 --> 00:09:39.600
at Johns Hopkins. Most of America has less
than $500 of cash in savings. They live a

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:45.620
very different life and healthcare has forgotten
them and we refer to them as if they've done

00:09:45.620 --> 00:09:51.390
something wrong. Like they've been irresponsible
and failed to get health insurance. No, these

00:09:51.390 --> 00:09:56.710
people have health insurance. Our research
shows that they're insured, they're working.

00:09:56.710 --> 00:10:02.230
The most common place they work is Walmart.
Number two is us postal workers, workers that

00:10:02.230 --> 00:10:10.220
Lowe's nurses and housekeepers at hospitals
being sued by their own facility. Uh, we did

00:10:10.220 --> 00:10:15.890
the expo say on Mary Washington Hospital.
We're now looking at UVA, which gets taxpayer

00:10:15.890 --> 00:10:21.790
funding and sues a lot of people. Memphis,
uh, the baptist system just highlighted and

00:10:21.790 --> 00:10:27.790
Propublica, uh, in New Mexico. So we're finding
hospitals around the country that are violating

00:10:27.790 --> 00:10:34.779
the public trust of our great medical profession.
We're calling them out. We're doing this@restoringmedicine.org.org

00:10:34.779 --> 00:10:39.720
and so far the first hospital that we talked
to has announced they are going to stop suing

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:40.720
patients.

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:45.980
Would someone have recourse to say that they,
they were sort of in a situation of, of duress

00:10:45.980 --> 00:10:50.380
when they were in the situation where they
sort of agreed to receive certain treatment

00:10:50.380 --> 00:10:54.830
because in certain emergency situations or
even urgent situations, you're, you're not

00:10:54.830 --> 00:10:59.200
exactly sort of commonly stepping back the
way you would. If you're thinking about choices

00:10:59.200 --> 00:11:03.540
for buying a car and making a decision. You're
in a situation where a doctor says this is

00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:08.029
the procedure you need. Then later you end
up getting sued for something which, which

00:11:08.029 --> 00:11:12.970
I would argue there is a sort of analogous
duress type of situation. Is that recourse

00:11:12.970 --> 00:11:17.260
that these sue lawsuit victims can, can sort
of try to employ?

00:11:17.260 --> 00:11:22.790
Yes, but David, to be honest, they're too
scared. They are how are served at home by

00:11:22.790 --> 00:11:28.020
a sheriff's deputy that wakes him up at seven
in the morning. We founded some cases and

00:11:28.020 --> 00:11:32.440
they're told to come to court and they come
to court and are asked a question, do you

00:11:32.440 --> 00:11:36.649
owe this money? They're afraid of perjuring
themselves. They don't really understand what's

00:11:36.649 --> 00:11:41.660
happening. They just say yes and then they
have to provide their routing number and account

00:11:41.660 --> 00:11:45.410
number in the back of the courthouse to the
hospital who pulls the money out of their

00:11:45.410 --> 00:11:49.630
savings or their paycheck has garnished or
Eileen has put on their home. We have been

00:11:49.630 --> 00:11:54.940
representing patients pro bono as best we
can. I will list my name as the medical expert

00:11:54.940 --> 00:12:01.440
on their case for free and I will argue to
the judge, the patient doesn't owe the money.

00:12:01.440 --> 00:12:06.580
I can't mow your lawn and charge you $50,000
without a contract.

00:12:06.580 --> 00:12:11.950
There's gotta be an agreement. Many people
are asking, I want to know the price of what

00:12:11.950 --> 00:12:17.680
it's going to cost to deliver a baby beforehand.
60% of medical care is shoppable. That is

00:12:17.680 --> 00:12:22.250
it's elective, right? No one's suggesting
we give you a price if you get shot and need

00:12:22.250 --> 00:12:27.220
to have a heart valve replaced in the, in
the trauma bay. Okay. We're talking about

00:12:27.220 --> 00:12:33.529
elective care hospitals get their act together,
physician groups, healthcare, the elite, the

00:12:33.529 --> 00:12:38.780
medical establishment, and figure out a way
to do honest and fair, transparent billing

00:12:38.780 --> 00:12:45.520
to patients and give them a price before the
service. Many of these patients sued, asked

00:12:45.520 --> 00:12:51.420
for a price and were told the runaround, the
typical run around. It's common in hospitals.

00:12:51.420 --> 00:12:57.930
Um, if you are somebody paying out of pocket
or out of network, which is a fast growing

00:12:57.930 --> 00:13:02.040
group of Americans and you need to deliver
a baby, don't you think you should be able

00:13:02.040 --> 00:13:06.340
to look up what it costs at different hospitals,
assuming it's an uncomplicated delivery.

00:13:06.340 --> 00:13:12.260
So we've argued to the judges. There is no
valid contract. People come to us in a hospital

00:13:12.260 --> 00:13:17.680
under duress. They are stressed. They're scared,
right? When I see a patient in the emergency

00:13:17.680 --> 00:13:23.340
room or in my clinic with who was told they
have cancer, there is one emotion that dominates

00:13:23.340 --> 00:13:28.360
their mind and that is they're petrified.
They're scared. We have historically been

00:13:28.360 --> 00:13:34.010
a refuge for people when they are hurting.
Now we've got patients in the community saying

00:13:34.010 --> 00:13:40.301
in Virginia, one woman said, I'm at home and
sick and scared to go to the hospital. They've

00:13:40.301 --> 00:13:47.240
sued me twice. That is violating this great
public trust that we've had. And I think doctors

00:13:47.240 --> 00:13:52.110
and nurses and everyone at a hospital are
starting to speak up. And we're seeing this

00:13:52.110 --> 00:13:56.270
now with our restoring medicine Facebook page
and other, uh, venues.

00:13:56.270 --> 00:14:00.921
The book is called the price we pay what broke
American health care and how to fix it. It's

00:14:00.921 --> 00:14:05.760
available now on Amazon and we've been speaking
with the books author, Dr Dr Marty McCarry.

00:14:05.760 --> 00:14:07.750
Thanks so much for talking to us about it.

00:14:07.750 --> 00:14:09.769
Does today great to be with

